00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.110
Welcome to Run Containers
on GCP's Serverless

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:21.180
Infrastructure.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.030
Well, you all know what
we are talking about here.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:27.000
We are talking about Cloud Run,
a product that we are really

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.210
excited to launch and that
we announced yesterday,

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.700
that allows you
to run containers

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.365
on all serverless
infrastructure.

00:00:35.365 --> 00:00:39.240
So first, what is serverless?

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:43.610
This is how we define
serverless at Google Cloud.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:45.950
The three characteristics
are that you do not

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:50.450
manage the infrastructure,
you have a managed security,

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:54.000
and you only pay for usage.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.770
If you take those three
characteristics, you can think,

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:02.550
that doesn't only apply
to compute products.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.459
And indeed, all
of those products

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:09.130
are, if we apply those
characteristics, by definition,

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:09.840
serverless.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.970
For example, if you
want to install data,

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.420
Firestore is an ideal
serverless database.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.250
If you want to do Cron
job, well, Cloud Scheduler

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.890
is an ideal way
to not worry about

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:25.920
where your Crons are running
in a fully-managed environment.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.400
And more precisely, we have
three serverless products,

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.970
including Cloud Run.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:34.980
So we are introducing
Cloud Run that

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:39.870
allows you to bring
serverless to containers.

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:43.730
So, Mike, why don't
you show us how easy

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:45.580
it is to deploy a
container to Cloud Run.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:46.622
Let's switch to the demo.

00:01:46.622 --> 00:01:48.992
MICHAEL HELMICK: So let's
switch over to the demo.

00:01:48.992 --> 00:01:50.450
We've shown this
demo several times

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:51.380
in several different sessions.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:52.570
So you may have seen it already.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:54.278
We're just going to
show you how quick it

00:01:54.278 --> 00:01:56.970
is to get a pre-built container
up and running in Cloud Run.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:00.390
So here I am in the Cloud
Run UI, in the Cloud console.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:03.240
So we're going to
hit Create Service.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:07.588
We've provided you an
example container right here.

00:02:07.588 --> 00:02:09.380
This is our official
Hello World container.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.063
It will pre-populate
the service name

00:02:11.063 --> 00:02:12.230
based on the container name.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:13.920
You're free to change
that if you like.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.340
It'll give you a
dropdown for locations.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.890
I promise, that list is
going to get longer soon.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:21.170
And then I'm going to
click Allow Unauthenticated

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:21.870
Invocations.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:24.170
So by default, services
in Cloud Run are private.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.150
We'll talk more about what that
actually means in a minute,

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:28.192
but I want to put this
container on the internet.

00:02:28.192 --> 00:02:32.180
I'm going to go
ahead and hit Create.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:33.885
It flips over to
the service display.

00:02:33.885 --> 00:02:35.098
I see creating service.

00:02:35.098 --> 00:02:37.140
So there's a couple of
cycles to go through here.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.723
It's going to say,
deploying revision,

00:02:38.723 --> 00:02:39.910
performing health check.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.910
So after we pull your
container into our system,

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:43.350
we actually start it up.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:44.100
It's already done.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:44.730
We start it up.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.480
Make sure that it can start.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.030
We return an error if it can't.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.006
And then we give you an URL.

00:02:50.006 --> 00:02:52.480
So right there, I
have an HTTPS URL.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:53.980
I can click over,
and that container

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.100
is serving in less
than 10 seconds.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.092
