00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:00.060
Thank you.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.560
I'll switch back
over to the slides.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.970
We'll tell you about
how this works.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:06.330
STEREN GIANNINI: So you
might [INAUDIBLE] that's

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:10.350
a great piece of technology,
but why would I use Cloud Run?

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:13.000
So let's go a little bit
more into the use cases.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.730
So we can split them
into two main categories.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:22.930
Always, you can run HTTP
request-driven workloads.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:26.970
So some of them, you will be
using them for public-facing

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:27.885
services--

00:03:27.885 --> 00:03:29.760
anything that is exposed
to the internet that

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.410
is serving things
over the internet,

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:37.360
for example, a website, or
exposing an API endpoint,

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:41.220
backend for your mobile app,
or just a simple web hook

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:44.430
for when you're receiving web
hook from GitHub or a chat bot,

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:46.140
for example.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:49.530
But you can also use Cloud
Run if you are breaking out

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:51.810
the monolith of your
application and are

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.330
using private microservices.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:57.720
In that case, those services
are not accessible by anybody

00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:58.540
on the internet.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:03.390
They are within your
service, your application.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:07.720
And all of those microservices
will be indefinitely scalable.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:12.330
So if one of them is not in use,
it will just not be scaled up.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:15.700
And it will be scaled
up when needed.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:17.850
You can do direct
microservice to microservice,

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.620
but also do asynchronous
task execution.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:23.190
And here, we've seen
many of our alpha testers

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:27.900
using Cloud Run to do
data transformation, image

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.410
processing, video processing,
document processing, as you

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:33.530
might have seen in
some other sessions.

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:36.670
And all of this, you can do as
asynchronous task execution,

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:43.020
where the task is going to
trigger the Cloud Run service.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:47.390
So as one of our
alpha testers says,

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:51.050
what is great about Cloud Run
is that because it accepts

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:53.060
any stateless
container, it allows

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:58.130
you to use your own custom tool
chains, both in terms of tool

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:01.790
chain to manipulate containers,
to build them, but also,

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:04.340
your tool chains
inside the container.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:07.370
In that case, Thomas
from Airbus is

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:09.680
using Cloud Run to
run his own custom

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:14.180
binary in a serverless way
to do some image processing.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.190
But the binary is not
something that you usually

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:18.340
can put in a
serverless environment.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:19.970
Except now, with
Cloud Run, because you

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:22.100
can give any
stateless container,

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.050
you can actually
give any software

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:28.280
to run on a serverless
environment.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:31.970
So in that session, we
are going to deep dive

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:35.900
into Cloud Run, in particular,
into the fully-managed version

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:37.720
of Cloud Run.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:39.970
So we are going to go around
the many characteristics

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:42.460
of the product, so that you
have a very good understanding

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:44.360
of it.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.740
All right, let's go.

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:52.560
The first-- containers,
containers and serverless,

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:57.190
two things that we don't
really think go well together.

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:59.640
That reminds me--
peanut butter and jelly.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:04.050
Who would have imagined that
mixing peanut butter and jelly

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:05.700
would be so delicious?

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:07.170
Well, it's the same.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:11.310
You mix the
serverless principles

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:13.110
with the flexibility
of containers,

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:15.720
and you get something
really powerful.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.710
And this is what Cloud Run does.

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:23.320
So containers-- let's
keep going a little bit.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:27.070
First, they will allow
you to use any programming

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:28.600
language you want.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:31.720
You are not interested
anymore about which language

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:34.210
you can use, because the
cloud provider decided to only

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:35.320
support those languages.

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:41.378
No, this time, you use
your custom languages.

00:06:41.378 --> 00:06:43.920
We are going to do a demo with
Elixir, which is not something

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:46.420
that you've probably seen a lot
on a serverless environment.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:51.110
I personally run shell
scripts on the Cloud Run.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:52.910
Along with your
favorite language,

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:56.180
you can bring any
language package,

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:58.680
but also, any OS package.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:03.060
So if you have a package from
Ubuntu you want to install,

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:05.430
you can do so.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:08.910
Along with these, you can
bring your own custom binaries

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:12.150
as long as they are compiled
for the right system.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:14.803
But that's really
something very powerful.

00:07:14.803 --> 00:07:16.720
You don't even need to
go from source anymore.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:18.990
If you have a binary
that does something,

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:21.710
put it into the container.

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:25.790
And as we all know, containers
have a very large ecosystem

00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:27.620
around them.

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:30.470
In particular, they are
very large ecosystems

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:33.380
of base images, some
of them maintained

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:36.690
by the official
language maintainers.

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:40.750
And all of this is available
on Docker Hub, for example.

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:44.110
And lastly, we have to
acknowledge that containers

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:47.410
have become an industry
standard, an industry

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:51.040
standard to package software,
to distribute software,

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:54.240
to test software, and
even to deploy software.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:57.010
It's, in my opinion, much
better than a zip file.

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:02.550
So, Mike is going to
talk to you about what

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:04.725
your containers needs to
meet to run on Cloud Run.

00:08:04.725 --> 00:08:05.850
MICHAEL HELMICK: Thank you.

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:08.407
So we have what we call the
container runtime contract.

00:08:08.407 --> 00:08:09.990
The container runtime
contract that we

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:12.000
use in Cloud Run
and Cloud Run on GKE

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.310
is the Knative container
runtime contract.

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:16.308
So this is an open
spec that we published.

00:08:16.308 --> 00:08:17.850
It defines just a
few things that you

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:19.830
need to do inside
your container for us

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:21.760
to be able to
automatically scale it.

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:24.930
So the first one is that we're
focused on stateless workloads.

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:27.150
What this means to you as
an application developer

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:28.920
is that you should
not rely on any given

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:31.200
instance of your
application living for more

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:32.380
than one request.

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:34.169
Now in practice,
on Cloud Run, we

00:08:34.169 --> 00:08:36.299
do reuse an instance
of your container,

00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:38.070
and we'll send multiple
requests to it.

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:40.350
But we'll also scale them
up and down as needed.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.480
So we're not a great
use case for, say,

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.520
running a mem cache
inside of your container.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:48.150
You can't guarantee it's
going to stay there.

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:51.990
With that, we require that
you listen on a specific port.

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:55.080
We pass in an environment
variable called port.

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:56.837
In practice, it's
usually 80/80, but you

00:08:56.837 --> 00:08:59.170
should be prepared for that
to change at any given time.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:00.680
That's part of the contract.

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:03.570
And we only support
HTTP 1.0, currently--

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:04.470
1.1.

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:06.420
We don't support
any streaming RPCs.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:09.780
There's some scaling issues
with persistent connections

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:12.690
that I'll be happy to talk about
more in depth after the talk.

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:14.190
And then instances
of your container

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:16.610
are only running when
handling the user requests.

00:09:16.610 --> 00:09:18.720
This is how we're able to
offer the scale that we

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:19.980
do on Cloud Run.

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:21.720
So between servicing
user requests,

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:24.960
we actually throttle your
CPU down to near zero,

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:26.940
so that you're not being billed.

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:30.323
Your container is
actually not running.

00:09:30.323 --> 00:09:32.240
So with that, I'm going
to show you some code.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:34.272
I'm going to show you
a demo that I've built.

00:09:34.272 --> 00:09:35.980
So let's switch over
to the demo machine.

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:37.420
I'll show that
URL several times,

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:40.010
so you can get the
source, if you like.

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:41.530
So Steren mentioned
this earlier.

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:43.750
I've written this demo
entirely in Elixir.

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:47.560
Elixir is a dynamic language
that runs on the Erlang VM.

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:50.245
I decided I'd throw some
random things at Cloud Run

00:09:50.245 --> 00:09:51.280
and see how we do.

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:53.900
I also happen to really
like functional languages.

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:56.730
So what I've written
here is a chat bot.

00:09:56.730 --> 00:09:58.560
It works with Google
Hangouts Chat.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:00.280
So if you have a G
Suite domain, this

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:02.470
is something you
could easily do.

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:03.740
It has a nice chat interface.

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:06.190
And of course, we're using
the power of Cloud Run

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:10.340
to generate memes,
because why not?

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:10.840
So create.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:19.358
I just have a simple
command Create.

00:10:19.358 --> 00:10:20.650
It's going to go create a meme.

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:22.983
This is actually two different
microservices running out

00:10:22.983 --> 00:10:23.800
on Cloud Run--

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:25.280
one to do the chat
bot interface,

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:26.510
one to generate the images.

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:29.302
It responds fairly quickly.

00:10:29.302 --> 00:10:31.010
But let's say here
that what I want to do

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:32.385
is, I want to--
this is right now

00:10:32.385 --> 00:10:33.790
a direct chat with the chat.

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:35.540
But let's say I want
to put this in rooms,

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:37.998
because of course, memes are
more fun if we're sharing them

00:10:37.998 --> 00:10:38.810
with other people.

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:41.060
So I want this to, when
it responds with the meme,

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:43.352
to put the author,
attribute this

00:10:43.352 --> 00:10:45.060
to the author of the
person who wrote it.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:48.683
So let's switch over
to Cloud Shell here.

00:10:48.683 --> 00:10:50.600
So I've got my environment
all up and running.

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:52.183
I want to show you
a couple of things.

00:10:52.183 --> 00:10:54.890
So the first is the
container contract

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:57.390
that I mentioned-- listening
on a specific port.

00:10:57.390 --> 00:11:01.670
So this is pretty easy to do in
most languages and frameworks.

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:04.910
For Elixir, this happens
to be how you specify

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:08.180
this in the cowboy config.

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:10.580
Tell it to pull it from
the system environment

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:12.290
variable called port.

00:11:12.290 --> 00:11:14.555
By default, this is
typically hardcoded.

00:11:14.555 --> 00:11:16.430
So you might find,
depending on your language

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:19.850
or your framework, it might
listen on 4000, by default,

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:21.777
4200, 8080, 8000.

00:11:21.777 --> 00:11:23.610
There's lots of different
variants in there,

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:26.130
so make sure you
get the right one.

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:30.500
The second thing is, I'm going
to show you the Docker file.

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:33.170
So there's loads, and loads of
examples out on the internet

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:34.550
for different Docker files.

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:36.050
What I've done here
is I've actually

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:38.630
created a six-staged Docker
build, which, I know,

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:39.710
sounds scary.

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:42.360
I've done that to leverage
caching in the build system.

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:44.830
So if I build this
from scratch, it

00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:47.570
takes about four minutes to
build using Google Cloud Build.

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:49.730
But with the caching, if
I just change one file,

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:52.710
I'm able to recycle and get a
new container within a minute,

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:55.920
then we can get that deployed
fairly quickly on Cloud Run.

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:57.740
So here I'm doing,
again, nothing funny.

00:11:57.740 --> 00:12:00.680
I'm pulling from the
Elixir 1.8 Alpine

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:03.125
image, which is maintained
by the Elixir community.

00:12:03.125 --> 00:12:05.365
Of course Alpine then
being a slimmed down image.

00:12:05.365 --> 00:12:06.740
What this gives
me is it gives me

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:10.460
the entire build chain that
I need to build and release

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:11.980
an Elixir application.

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:13.730
Again, there's a couple
of different steps

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:15.105
here with different
layers, where

00:12:15.105 --> 00:12:17.520
I'm copying specific
files, looking for hey,

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:20.570
if the mixed build file has
changed, execute this layer.

00:12:20.570 --> 00:12:22.430
If it hasn't, used
the cache one.

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:23.810
Move on to the next thing.

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:26.690
It's compiling all
the static assets,

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:28.753
compiling my application.

00:12:28.753 --> 00:12:30.920
And then what I'm doing is,
in the last stage there,

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:34.460
I'm pulling a vanilla
Alpine 39 image,

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:37.790
copying over just the
binary, just the Erlang

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:41.457
VM, my application, and
then a simple start command.

00:12:41.457 --> 00:12:43.040
So I actually have
a very small image.

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:44.660
This is less than
40 megabytes when

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:47.810
pushed to GCR, which means that
it deploys and starts up very

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:49.600
quickly.

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:51.700
And then the bot code
itself, for those of you who

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:54.170
are interested and
want to dig in later,

00:12:54.170 --> 00:12:56.990
I have a simple
configuration here.

00:12:56.990 --> 00:13:01.640
This is a list of tuples
with strings to look for

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:04.120
and functions to dispatch to.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:05.870
And then in the bot
here, this is the code

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:08.340
that we're going
to actually change.

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:15.220
So in the Create function,
create a meme right here.

00:13:15.220 --> 00:13:17.585
So in the message that I get--

00:13:17.585 --> 00:13:19.290
oh, that's create templates.

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:20.770
Let's see-- create meme.

00:13:20.770 --> 00:13:22.480
In the message that
I get from Hangouts

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:23.980
Chat has some
additional information

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:25.640
about who sent the request.

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:28.540
So I'm just going to pull the
sender name from that JSON

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:36.830
that I get in and say,
created by sender name.

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:37.955
So it's simple code change.

00:13:42.188 --> 00:13:44.480
I'm just going to--oh, I
don't have Mix installed here.

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:45.880
So what I'm going to do is I'm
going to actually send this

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:47.040
over to Google Cloud Build.

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:55.570
G Cloud Build, Submit, give it
a tag for where I want it to go.

00:13:55.570 --> 00:13:59.380
We support tags
on GCR, so if you

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:01.810
want to tag something as
v1, or switch to a v2,

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:04.000
maybe switch between
them, we support that.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:07.240
At deploy time, we resolve
the current value of that tag

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:10.180
to the image digests, so that
if we ever had to redeploy that

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:12.940
in the future-- which is a
feature that's coming soon--

00:14:12.940 --> 00:14:16.007
we would pull the exact
same image and not

00:14:16.007 --> 00:14:17.090
the new one from the tags.

00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:19.130
So I'm going to go ahead
and hit Submit on that.

00:14:19.130 --> 00:14:21.787
Like I said, this takes
about a minute or so.

00:14:21.787 --> 00:14:23.620
So we're going to switch
back to the slides,

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:24.940
and we're going to
talk rather than let

00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:27.398
you watch the build output,
because it's not very exciting.

00:14:28.945 --> 00:14:30.320
STEREN GIANNINI:
Thank you, Mike.

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:30.940
MICHAEL HELMICK:
Thank you, Steren.

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:33.490
STEREN GIANNINI: So let's
recap what's happened here.

00:14:33.490 --> 00:14:36.600
We had source code, and
we packaged all of this

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.550
into a container image.

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:42.990
To do so, the de-facto tool
that everybody has heard of

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:46.890
is Docker, the Docker command
line, Docker build -t,

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:47.610
Docker push.

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:49.110
This is something,
too, that you can

00:14:49.110 --> 00:14:52.460
use to build containers
images locally, on your CI

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:54.840
system, whatever you want,

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:58.170
As Mike has used,
Google Cloud also offers

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:01.140
Build as a service if you
want, where you only pay

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:02.670
for when the Build is running.

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:05.980
And when nothing happens,
it's all managed for you.

00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:07.280
So this is Cloud Build.

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:09.810
For Cloud Build, you
can do a Docker build.

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:13.110
This is what Mike has done, but
you can also do more complex,

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:16.380
multi-stage builds
with Cloud build.YAML.

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:18.570
Feel free to look at
the documentation.

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:21.150
It's quite powerful actually,
because Cloud Build at the end

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:24.510
can build containers that can
do much more, like deploying

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:26.460
or executing actions.

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:30.550
So we can use Cloud Build
for continuous delivery, too.

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:33.280
Personally, I recommend
two other tools

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:34.150
to build containers.

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:36.370
If you are Java
developers, I heard

00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:38.560
that Jib is very, very good.

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:44.080
So Jib will allow you to, by
importing a Maven package,

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:46.060
build a container
for your Java app

00:15:46.060 --> 00:15:48.850
without even having
Docker installed locally.

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:50.320
So then you use
it at one command,

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:52.690
and you have a container
from a Maven package

00:15:52.690 --> 00:15:55.960
that you installed
from that XML.

00:15:55.960 --> 00:15:58.660
Also, personally, I'm
a fan of buildpacks.

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:03.340
Buildpacks will help you go from
a source in a given language

00:16:03.340 --> 00:16:04.480
to a container.

00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:07.030
The recipe to transform
your source into a container

00:16:07.030 --> 00:16:09.580
is contained inside
the buildpack.

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:12.520
And so at the end, you run one
command, which is a pack build.

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:15.400
And you will end up with
a container at the end,

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:19.150
without even having to worry
about writing the Docker file.

00:16:19.150 --> 00:16:24.900
And as I said, I encourage
you, with containers,

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:27.390
to leverage this continuous
integration systems

00:16:27.390 --> 00:16:28.830
and continuous delivery.

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:31.470
That means that you build
your container once,

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:33.810
then you run your test on it.

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:35.520
Then you deploy it to staging.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:37.230
Then you deploy
it to production.

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:39.000
But it's always
the same container.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:41.520
It's the one you've
built once and you tested

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:42.820
that goes into production.

00:16:42.820 --> 00:16:45.360
It's not some source that
are rebuilt every time.

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:48.390
So we are very sure that it's
really the right thing that

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:49.490
goes to prod.

00:16:49.490 --> 00:16:51.330
And so for this, as
I said, Cloud Build

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:53.580
can be a very easy
tool to do so,

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:56.160
but of course, Jenkins
and many other tools

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:59.490
are helping you do that.

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:02.220
Then once you have
your container image,

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:07.020
as we've seen in the first demo,
you can deploy it to Cloud Run.

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:08.810
So you can do it from
the user interface.

00:17:08.810 --> 00:17:10.310
You can do it from
the command line,

00:17:10.310 --> 00:17:13.050
gcloud beta run deploy
dash dash image.

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:15.960
And so here, you go from
your container image

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:18.180
in Google Cloud
Container Registry

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:21.030
to Cloud Run with one command.

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:23.800
On Cloud Run, you get an
endpoint to invoke it.

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:26.520
And all of this, if you
remember from the first demo,

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:31.720
happens very fast, in
just a few seconds.

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:34.083
I think, once it's
on Cloud Run--

00:17:34.083 --> 00:17:36.000
MICHAEL HELMICK: Yeah,
once it's on Cloud Run,

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:38.413
we give you a URL.

00:17:38.413 --> 00:17:40.080
Everything you deploy
will automatically

00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:42.210
get a stable URL
for that service

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:46.265
under the run.app
domain, secured with SSL.

00:17:46.265 --> 00:17:48.640
If that doesn't work for you,
we offer free custom domain

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:49.140
mapping.

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:51.760
So any domain you own, you
can bring your base domain

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:53.680
or a subdomain off
that base domain,

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:56.560
map it to an individual
Cloud Run service.

00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:58.660
And with that, we give
you a free SSL certificate

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:02.040
that we will auto-renew
for you about a month

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:02.790
before it expires.

00:18:02.790 --> 00:18:04.450
We give a little
bit of leeway there.

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:06.820
Free certificates
from Let's Encrypt.

00:18:06.820 --> 00:18:09.100
We also offer Firebase
Hosting integration.

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:11.230
Firebase Hosting allows
you to add static content

00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:14.600
through a CDM, or you can
do cached API requests.

00:18:14.600 --> 00:18:18.130
So if you are serving something,
where the answer to the API

00:18:18.130 --> 00:18:20.680
doesn't change very frequently,
doing a Firebase hosting

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:22.860
integration is potentially
a good solution for you

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:25.520
in that space.

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:28.100
So the container
built. I checked

00:18:28.100 --> 00:18:29.547
while Steren was talking.

00:18:29.547 --> 00:18:31.130
So let's switch back
over to the demo.

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:33.990
We're going to actually go
ahead and deploy this change.

00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:37.130
So you can see here that it took
a minute and 42 seconds, which

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:39.810
is less than half of the
time for a complete build.

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:40.620
So that's good.

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:45.050
I encourage you to look at the
caching functionality of Google

00:18:45.050 --> 00:18:46.490
Cloud Build.

00:18:46.490 --> 00:18:48.240
And it gives me the output here.

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:48.875
So let's see.

00:18:52.983 --> 00:18:53.900
So I'm going to go in.

00:18:53.900 --> 00:18:54.692
I'm going to cheat.

00:18:54.692 --> 00:18:56.570
I've got the command
already there.

00:18:56.570 --> 00:18:58.460
So g cloud beta run deploy.

00:18:58.460 --> 00:19:00.800
The name of the service,
dash dash image, the name

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:03.228
of the image, hit Deploy.

00:19:03.228 --> 00:19:04.270
So I'm going to hit that.

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:05.720
While that's going, I'm
going to talk about a couple

00:19:05.720 --> 00:19:07.887
of other things you can do
on the command line here.

00:19:07.887 --> 00:19:09.860
You can specify the memory
for your containers.

00:19:09.860 --> 00:19:13.140
So the default memory is
256 megabytes per instance.

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:16.130
You can scale that up to
2 gigabytes for instance,

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:18.810
depending on what
you need to do.

00:19:18.810 --> 00:19:20.995
I've already specified
512 for this container,

00:19:20.995 --> 00:19:23.120
but I don't need to specify
