00:20:53.043 --> 00:20:55.460
So let's switch back to the
slides, talk a little bit more

00:20:55.460 --> 00:20:57.377
about some of the stuff
that's going on there.

00:20:59.515 --> 00:21:02.140
At a high level, I want to talk
a little bit about the resource

00:21:02.140 --> 00:21:03.190
model that we used.

00:21:03.190 --> 00:21:05.500
I mentioned, it's the
Knative Serving API.

00:21:05.500 --> 00:21:07.370
The top level object
in there is service.

00:21:07.370 --> 00:21:09.940
So you create a service.

00:21:09.940 --> 00:21:12.610
Requests come into a service.

00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:14.230
Every time you
change that service,

00:21:14.230 --> 00:21:17.410
you get a new revision, so
revision 1, revision 2, so on

00:21:17.410 --> 00:21:18.800
and so forth.

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:21.040
Only one revision is
serving at a time.

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:24.410
So if you dig under the hood
of the Knative service object,

00:21:24.410 --> 00:21:26.028
there's a couple
that we call modes.

00:21:26.028 --> 00:21:27.820
There's run latest
mode, which is currently

00:21:27.820 --> 00:21:28.820
what Cloud Run supports.

00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:31.153
There are also some other
modes, like release mode, that

00:21:31.153 --> 00:21:32.410
lets you do a traffic split.

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:35.980
Those things are coming soon.

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:39.130
When requests come in for
the current latest revision,

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:40.930
we scale the number
of container instances

00:21:40.930 --> 00:21:43.600
of that revision that are needed
to handle the incoming traffic.

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:45.318
And we scale that
both up and down,

00:21:45.318 --> 00:21:47.110
and you're only billed
when you're actually

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:48.145
handling a user request.

00:21:50.507 --> 00:21:52.090
In the Knative
resource model, there's

00:21:52.090 --> 00:21:53.180
a couple of other objects.

00:21:53.180 --> 00:21:56.350
There's route and configuration
and also the revision resource,

00:21:56.350 --> 00:21:57.220
in particular.

00:21:57.220 --> 00:21:59.950
We do expose all of
those Knative objects

00:21:59.950 --> 00:22:00.950
in read-only mode.

00:22:00.950 --> 00:22:03.910
So you can really dig in and
see exactly what's going on

00:22:03.910 --> 00:22:05.410
with your declarative
config, but we

00:22:05.410 --> 00:22:07.827
do require all changes right
now to go through the service

00:22:07.827 --> 00:22:10.370
object.

00:22:10.370 --> 00:22:13.370
I showed you the preview before,
but again, Knative Serving API.

00:22:13.370 --> 00:22:15.200
If you go to GitHub.com
slash Knative

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:17.810
slash serving, you can see
the entire Knative Serving

00:22:17.810 --> 00:22:20.510
project, all of the issues,
designs, what you've written,

00:22:20.510 --> 00:22:21.490
things like that.

00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:24.930
And then, again, under the hood,
you have the YAML file here.

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:27.380
So currently, we do
not support kubectl.

00:22:27.380 --> 00:22:29.630
We cannot take requests
through that same declarative

00:22:29.630 --> 00:22:30.130
interface.

00:22:30.130 --> 00:22:34.480
That support is also
coming later this year.

00:22:34.480 --> 00:22:36.520
But the same interface,
so the same config,

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:38.680
you can take from Cloud Run.

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:40.540
Move it over to
Cloud Run on GKE.

00:22:40.540 --> 00:22:45.120
Get the same container up and
running in that environment.

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:47.100
Cloud Run is a regional service.

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:49.043
Each service lives in
a different region.

00:22:49.043 --> 00:22:51.210
You can deploy the same
service to multiple regions,

00:22:51.210 --> 00:22:51.710
if you like.

00:22:51.710 --> 00:22:54.420
We do not currently offer
a global load balancing

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:56.210
over those regions.

00:22:56.210 --> 00:22:58.188
And right now, we're
live in US central one,

00:22:58.188 --> 00:22:59.730
but we have a bunch
more regions that

00:22:59.730 --> 00:23:02.520
are going to be coming
online very, very soon.

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:05.287
It's not going to be a
long wait, I promise.

00:23:05.287 --> 00:23:06.870
So Steren's going
to talk a little bit

00:23:06.870 --> 00:23:09.507
about the invoker permissions
and private services.

00:23:09.507 --> 00:23:11.340
STEREN GIANNINI: Yeah,
let's spend some time

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:14.040
on a very important
concept of Cloud Run.

00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:17.250
So as Mike described,
requests are coming in,

00:23:17.250 --> 00:23:20.460
and they are reaching
your service or not.

00:23:20.460 --> 00:23:22.470
Because in front
of your service,

00:23:22.470 --> 00:23:26.490
there is a notification
layer that you

00:23:26.490 --> 00:23:29.910
use IAM to control
who has the permission

00:23:29.910 --> 00:23:32.050
to invoke that service.

00:23:32.050 --> 00:23:35.820
So for example, it can be
anybody with all users.

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:38.820
It can be a specific
user or even

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:41.170
a specific service account.

00:23:41.170 --> 00:23:46.530
So this feature is key to
doing public or private

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:47.370
microservices.

00:23:47.370 --> 00:23:50.250
And let's see a few use cases
how you can use that feature.

00:23:50.250 --> 00:23:52.420
You will see, it's
quite powerful.

00:23:52.420 --> 00:23:55.800
So the easiest one,
the most obvious one

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:57.270
is you have a Cloud Run service.

00:23:57.270 --> 00:24:00.450
You want to expose it to
the internet to anybody.

00:24:00.450 --> 00:24:03.060
In that case, there is no
authentication required

00:24:03.060 --> 00:24:04.660
to invoke your service.

00:24:04.660 --> 00:24:07.680
How you achieve that is by
giving all users the right

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:09.390
to invoke your service.

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:11.550
All right, this is
a public service.

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:13.950
Now, if, within
your architecture,

00:24:13.950 --> 00:24:17.270
you have one microservice that
calls another microservice,

00:24:17.270 --> 00:24:21.500
for example, your frontend
now invokes your backend,

00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:25.370
while your backend is not
exposed to the internet.

00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:28.730
So to be sure that
only the frontend can

00:24:28.730 --> 00:24:31.610
call your backend
and not anybody

00:24:31.610 --> 00:24:35.000
or not even your development
team on production,

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:36.590
well, this is how you can do it.

00:24:36.590 --> 00:24:41.000
You can give the invoke
permission to the frontend

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:42.830
service account.

00:24:42.830 --> 00:24:45.380
Because each Cloud Run service
is associated with a service

00:24:45.380 --> 00:24:46.520
account.

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:49.160
And so then when your
frontend calls your backend,

00:24:49.160 --> 00:24:52.760
it has to do it by
sending with the request

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:55.520
a special header to
authorize the request.

00:24:55.520 --> 00:24:58.590
Once you do this, only the
frontend can call the backend.

00:24:58.590 --> 00:25:00.350
So all of this is
leveraging IAM,

00:25:00.350 --> 00:25:02.410
something you
probably know already

00:25:02.410 --> 00:25:04.590
if you are using Google Cloud.

00:25:04.590 --> 00:25:06.540
But that's not it.

00:25:06.540 --> 00:25:09.420
If you want to do
asynchronous event processing,

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:12.840
I recommend you to use Pub/Sub
to create a topic, and then

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:14.160
a subscription.

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:16.080
And then here, what
you do is that you

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:19.140
give to the subscription an
identity with the service

00:25:19.140 --> 00:25:19.850
account.

00:25:19.850 --> 00:25:22.680
This is the thing
you see on the top.

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:27.390
And then your service, you only
allow that Pub/Sub identity

00:25:27.390 --> 00:25:29.020
to invoke your service.

00:25:29.020 --> 00:25:32.070
So once again, your service
is completely private,

00:25:32.070 --> 00:25:36.460
and only Pub/Sub has the
ability to invoke it.

00:25:36.460 --> 00:25:40.180
That's how you make asynchronous
processing super-secure.

00:25:40.180 --> 00:25:42.060
So in that case, I
give the invoker role

00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:44.610
to the Pub/Sub service account.

00:25:44.610 --> 00:25:47.400
And by the way, when you
use Pub/Sub in Cloud Run,

00:25:47.400 --> 00:25:50.550
the run dot app URLs are
already validated for you.

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:52.140
You don't need to
do a verification

00:25:52.140 --> 00:25:54.000
step of ownership of that URL.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:56.500
We already know its yours.

00:25:56.500 --> 00:25:57.580
This is Pub/Sub.

00:25:57.580 --> 00:26:01.060
One of my other
tools in my tool belt

00:26:01.060 --> 00:26:05.770
is Cloud Tasks, which
as of maybe yesterday,

00:26:05.770 --> 00:26:08.340
is now supporting HTTP targets.

00:26:08.340 --> 00:26:11.020
So Cloud Tasks is a
queuing mechanism.

00:26:11.020 --> 00:26:13.180
You can send tasks to
a queue, and the queue

00:26:13.180 --> 00:26:16.810
will ensure that the tasks
are processed by pushing them

00:26:16.810 --> 00:26:20.380
to a Cloud Run service or
to an App Engine service.

00:26:20.380 --> 00:26:25.390
And in the case of a Cloud
Run service, it's the same.

00:26:25.390 --> 00:26:27.760
You give the service
account of Cloud Tasks

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:30.050
the permission to
invoke your service.

00:26:30.050 --> 00:26:32.910
It's like Pub/Sub.

00:26:32.910 --> 00:26:36.390
One other very useful
tool that you should all

00:26:36.390 --> 00:26:40.310
know about that exists in
GCP is Cloud Scheduler.

00:26:40.310 --> 00:26:43.320
Cloud Scheduler is the most
simplest tool you can think of.

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:45.470
It's Cron as a service.

00:26:45.470 --> 00:26:46.860
And here, it's the same.

00:26:46.860 --> 00:26:50.635
You just say, I only execute
that service once a day.

00:26:50.635 --> 00:26:52.260
Cloud Scheduler will
take care of that.

00:26:52.260 --> 00:26:54.330
Once a day, it
will send a request

00:26:54.330 --> 00:26:56.050
to your Cloud Run service.

00:26:56.050 --> 00:26:59.580
And again, you can
leverage IAM to ensure

00:26:59.580 --> 00:27:02.760
that only Cloud Scheduler is
able to invoke your service

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:04.650
and not exposing
it to the internet.

00:27:04.650 --> 00:27:08.080
So same-- you give the invoker
role to the Cloud Scheduler

00:27:08.080 --> 00:27:11.110
service account.

00:27:11.110 --> 00:27:16.243
Let's see a demo of how
Cloud Run can actually scale.

00:27:16.243 --> 00:27:18.410
MICHAEL HELMICK: Yeah, so
let's switch over to demo.

00:27:18.410 --> 00:27:21.200
What it is, I'm going to show
a demo of a service going

00:27:21.200 --> 00:27:24.530
from zero clients to
15,000 concurrent clients

00:27:24.530 --> 00:27:26.750
over the course of 60 seconds.

00:27:26.750 --> 00:27:30.950
Each of these is
hitting a worker job

00:27:30.950 --> 00:27:33.260
that I wrote that
simulates a real workload,

00:27:33.260 --> 00:27:34.360
so it burns some CPU.

00:27:34.360 --> 00:27:37.648
It sleeps to simulate, network
I/O, burn some CPU again.

00:27:37.648 --> 00:27:38.648
So I'm going to hit Run.

00:27:43.630 --> 00:27:48.345
This particular worker is using
a concurrency setting of 80.

00:27:48.345 --> 00:27:49.720
And when I get to
the next slide,

00:27:49.720 --> 00:27:51.637
I'm going to tell you
exactly what that means.

00:27:51.637 --> 00:27:53.750
So our test is up and
running, off to the races.

00:27:53.750 --> 00:27:55.690
So the linear line
that's going up

00:27:55.690 --> 00:27:59.920
is the number of clients
that are hitting the service.

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:01.880
The blue line is the latency.

00:28:01.880 --> 00:28:03.880
So I would expect the
latency to be a little bit

00:28:03.880 --> 00:28:06.338
variable, as we scale up the
number of container instances.

00:28:06.338 --> 00:28:10.750
But then it should level out
as we get to a steady state.

00:28:10.750 --> 00:28:13.140
So up to 6,000 clients.

00:28:13.140 --> 00:28:15.060
They should be sending
about 2 QPS each.

00:28:15.060 --> 00:28:18.420
So as we cross
8,000 clients here,

00:28:18.420 --> 00:28:22.170
that means we're handling
about 16,000 QPS in 30 seconds

00:28:22.170 --> 00:28:24.900
after starting at 0,
where 0, the service

00:28:24.900 --> 00:28:27.995
was completely idle.

00:28:27.995 --> 00:28:30.270
It's crossed 10,000,
so we're on the way.

00:28:30.270 --> 00:28:34.030
Up again to 15,000 over
the course of 60 seconds.

00:28:34.030 --> 00:28:36.850
We've successfully handled
over half a million requests.

00:28:36.850 --> 00:28:39.220
Just crossed 600,000
successful requests.

00:28:39.220 --> 00:28:42.320
There's a couple
of errors in there.

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:43.820
Sometimes we do see
some errors when

00:28:43.820 --> 00:28:45.695
you send a whole bunch
of load really quickly

00:28:45.695 --> 00:28:48.070
and have to scale up.

00:28:48.070 --> 00:28:51.240
So we got 15,000 clients.

00:28:51.240 --> 00:28:53.430
We successfully handled
over 800,000 requests.

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:56.070
Again, this scaled
up from nothing

00:28:56.070 --> 00:28:58.632
to a whole bunch of
instances very, very quickly.

00:28:58.632 --> 00:29:00.090
So let's switch
back to the slides.

00:29:00.090 --> 00:29:01.050
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:01.050 --> 00:29:06.738
Thank you Can we switch
back to the slides please?

00:29:06.738 --> 00:29:08.780
Talk a little bit about
the concurrency settings.

00:29:08.780 --> 00:29:12.540
So we showed some stuff
with automatic scaling.

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:13.750
Let's talk about concurrency.

00:29:13.750 --> 00:29:16.590
So in many serverless
applications

00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:19.710
today, concurrency setting
is one or, we call,

00:29:19.710 --> 00:29:20.610
single concurrency.

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:22.770
That means that any
instance is only

00:29:22.770 --> 00:29:24.860
going to be handling
one request at a time.

00:29:24.860 --> 00:29:26.970
So AWS Lambda and
Google Cloud Functions

00:29:26.970 --> 00:29:29.560
both use this single
concurrency model.

00:29:29.560 --> 00:29:31.560
What that means is that
you may have to start up

00:29:31.560 --> 00:29:32.768
a whole bunch more instances.

00:29:32.768 --> 00:29:34.890
You might experience a
lot more cold starts.

00:29:34.890 --> 00:29:37.920
In Cloud Run, we let you
have multi-concurrency

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:40.260
into a single
container instance.

00:29:40.260 --> 00:29:42.180
So we go up to 80.

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:44.687
You can specify, actually,
any number from 1 to 1,000,

00:29:44.687 --> 00:29:46.770
because that's what Knative
allows you to specify.

00:29:46.770 --> 00:29:48.550
But the limit is 80.

00:29:48.550 --> 00:29:50.425
I'll be open with you here.

00:29:50.425 --> 00:29:52.550
And what this allows you
to do is optimize resource

00:29:52.550 --> 00:29:53.140
consumption.

00:29:53.140 --> 00:29:57.660
So if you have a service that
makes lots of network calls,

00:29:57.660 --> 00:30:00.480
there's no reason not
to put multiple requests

00:30:00.480 --> 00:30:02.010
into that container at once--

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:03.570
avoid the cold start--

00:30:03.570 --> 00:30:04.800
unless you do optimized cost.

00:30:04.800 --> 00:30:08.380
You're only paying for the time
that your container is running.

00:30:08.380 --> 00:30:11.670
So if you're able to bin pack
and get a lot of the shared

00:30:11.670 --> 00:30:14.100
CPU in there, you can actually
end up with a lower cost

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:15.060
overall.

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:17.850
If you have a
CPU-bound workload,

00:30:17.850 --> 00:30:21.540
if you are you using that CPU
at 100% for the entire duration

00:30:21.540 --> 00:30:23.288
of the user request,
single concurrency

00:30:23.288 --> 00:30:25.080
probably actually makes
more sense for you.

00:30:25.080 --> 00:30:28.390
So there's a lot of variability
in what you actually need.

00:30:28.390 --> 00:30:31.210
And you can set this to
any integer along the way.

00:30:31.210 --> 00:30:33.900
So what I've done
is I've run a couple

00:30:33.900 --> 00:30:37.710
of load tests with different
concurrency settings here.

00:30:37.710 --> 00:30:40.650
So you can see here, with 400
clients sending three QPS--

00:30:40.650 --> 00:30:43.020
three requests per second--

00:30:43.020 --> 00:30:45.420
the number of instances I
needed on a single concurrency

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:46.950
was just over 500.

00:30:46.950 --> 00:30:49.320
So it was about 540 or so.

00:30:49.320 --> 00:30:51.990
And that was able to scale
up very quickly, as you

00:30:51.990 --> 00:30:52.990
can see here.

00:30:52.990 --> 00:30:55.750
As the traffic drops off, those
instances start to go away.

00:30:55.750 --> 00:30:58.290
So again, you're only paying
for when it's actually

00:30:58.290 --> 00:31:00.180
handling requests.

00:31:00.180 --> 00:31:03.270
Run that same test with
a concurrency of 80,

00:31:03.270 --> 00:31:05.370
I take out about 150 instances.

00:31:05.370 --> 00:31:09.210
So I don't know exactly what
the cost was between those two

00:31:09.210 --> 00:31:11.040
tests, but I know
this one was cheaper.

00:31:11.040 --> 00:31:13.810
There's less CPU time,
less instances involved.

00:31:13.810 --> 00:31:15.450
So this is truly a
pay-for-what-you-use

00:31:15.450 --> 00:31:19.137
experience, rapid scale
up, rapid scale down.

00:31:19.137 --> 00:31:20.595
Steren's going to
talk a little bit

00:31:20.595 --> 00:31:23.770
about how you can actually
debug and run these things, too.

00:31:23.770 --> 00:31:25.072
It's important things.

00:31:25.072 --> 00:31:26.530
STEREN GIANNINI:
So out of the box,

00:31:26.530 --> 00:31:29.230
as you would expect from
any serverless platform,

00:31:29.230 --> 00:31:31.690
we give you visibility
into your services

00:31:31.690 --> 00:31:34.320
by integrating with
Stackdriver monitoring.

00:31:34.320 --> 00:31:37.870
You see all of your request
logs, application logs,

00:31:37.870 --> 00:31:41.860
[INAUDIBLE] dev logs
into Stackdriver Logging.

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:44.530
And when you use Stackdriver
Logging, as you might know,

00:31:44.530 --> 00:31:47.500
your exceptions are going
to be automatically captured

00:31:47.500 --> 00:31:50.610
and grouped into error
groups into Stackdriver error

00:31:50.610 --> 00:31:51.710
reporting.

00:31:51.710 --> 00:31:54.040
All of this is out
of the box as soon

00:31:54.040 --> 00:31:56.270
as you start using Cloud Run.

00:31:56.270 --> 00:31:58.720
We are also partnering
with Datadog,

00:31:58.720 --> 00:32:05.220
who are supporting Cloud Run
as a monitoring solution.

00:32:05.220 --> 00:32:09.300
So as you've seen in Mike's
demo, Cloud Run scales--

00:32:09.300 --> 00:32:12.150
scales up and scales down.

00:32:12.150 --> 00:32:17.490
It's very important to know that
each of your Cloud Run services

00:32:17.490 --> 00:32:21.720
will be automatically scaled
to as many container instances

00:32:21.720 --> 00:32:24.300
as needed to handle
the incoming requests.

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:27.730
The concurrency is on each
of those container instance.

00:32:27.730 --> 00:32:29.940
But the container
instance can go very high,

00:32:29.940 --> 00:32:32.110
like you've seen in Mike's demo.

00:32:32.110 --> 00:32:36.350
I think it went up to 500
when you had concurrency of 1.

00:32:36.350 --> 00:32:39.390
But what does that
mean for your bills?

00:32:39.390 --> 00:32:44.030
Well, what you pay is not the
number of scheduled instances.

00:32:44.030 --> 00:32:48.250
What you pay for is
the exact CPU, memory,

00:32:48.250 --> 00:32:51.030
and number of requests
that you are actually

00:32:51.030 --> 00:32:56.890
using down to a granularity
of a 100 milliseconds.

00:32:56.890 --> 00:33:02.350
So what that means is that the
auto-scaling is our problem.

00:33:02.350 --> 00:33:04.450
You only pay when there
is a request coming

00:33:04.450 --> 00:33:06.320
to your services.

00:33:06.320 --> 00:33:08.560
So let's take an example.

00:33:08.560 --> 00:33:11.080
Let's look at one
of those instances.

00:33:11.080 --> 00:33:13.630
We can have many of them,
but let's look at one.

00:33:13.630 --> 00:33:16.760
This instance will
consume CPU and memory.

00:33:16.760 --> 00:33:20.530
But when are we charging you for
the CPU and memory depends on

00:33:20.530 --> 00:33:22.610
if it is processing
requests or not.

00:33:22.610 --> 00:33:25.580
So on the x-axis, it's the time.

00:33:25.580 --> 00:33:26.520
But let's see.

00:33:26.520 --> 00:33:29.560
A first request arrives
and is returned.

00:33:29.560 --> 00:33:31.960
Another request arrives
and is returned.

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:34.170
This can happen,
because, as you know,

00:33:34.170 --> 00:33:36.547
Cloud Run has
multiple concurrency.

00:33:36.547 --> 00:33:38.380
So on Cloud Functions,
it's only one request

00:33:38.380 --> 00:33:40.090
at a time in a given instance.

00:33:40.090 --> 00:33:42.010
But on Cloud Run,
two requests can

00:33:42.010 --> 00:33:45.050
come at the same time
on a given instance.

00:33:45.050 --> 00:33:48.020
When that happens, what
we are charging you for

00:33:48.020 --> 00:33:51.280
is the beginning of
the first request up

00:33:51.280 --> 00:33:53.200
to the end of the last request.

00:33:53.200 --> 00:33:55.690
Outside of this, you
are charged zero.

00:33:55.690 --> 00:33:58.570
You don't pay for anything
outside of the processing

00:33:58.570 --> 00:33:59.200
of the request.

00:33:59.200 --> 00:34:01.360
You only pay for
CPU memory that you

00:34:01.360 --> 00:34:04.600
use when your containers
are processing requests.

00:34:04.600 --> 00:34:06.520
So from your perspective,
it's literally

00:34:06.520 --> 00:34:08.139
scaled down to zero instantly.

00:34:08.139 --> 00:34:11.110
From our perspective, we might
keep some things warm in case

00:34:11.110 --> 00:34:12.370
more requests arrive.

00:34:12.370 --> 00:34:14.750
But really, from the
perspective of your bill,

00:34:14.750 --> 00:34:19.760
you stop paying as soon as
the requests are returned.

00:34:19.760 --> 00:34:22.820
And how can we achieve
that magical scaling?

00:34:22.820 --> 00:34:25.010
Mike is going to
tell you a bit more.

00:34:25.010 --> 00:34:25.790
MICHAEL HELMICK:
There's lots of magic.

00:34:25.790 --> 00:34:27.207
I'm going to talk
about one piece.

00:34:27.207 --> 00:34:28.429
We're talking about gVisor.

00:34:28.429 --> 00:34:30.500
gVisor is the container
sandbox runtime

00:34:30.500 --> 00:34:31.969
that we use on Cloud Run.

00:34:31.969 --> 00:34:34.370
It's an open source
project run by Google.

00:34:34.370 --> 00:34:36.770
gVisor.dev is where you can
get a lot more information

00:34:36.770 --> 00:34:38.719
about that.

00:34:38.719 --> 00:34:40.969
It provides a secure
container isolation boundary.

00:34:40.969 --> 00:34:44.304
So we're able to start these
instances extremely fast,

00:34:44.304 --> 00:34:44.929
tear them down.

00:34:44.929 --> 00:34:47.060
It's very cheap to do.

00:34:47.060 --> 00:34:48.980
One thing about gVisor--

00:34:48.980 --> 00:34:50.699
not all sys calls
are implemented.

00:34:50.699 --> 00:34:53.330
So you might run into some
unimplemented sys call

00:34:53.330 --> 00:34:55.888
warnings, which we put
into your Stackdriver logs.

00:34:55.888 --> 00:34:57.680
So if you find that
your application is not

00:34:57.680 --> 00:34:59.138
working the way
you expect, I would

00:34:59.138 --> 00:35:00.490
encourage you to go look there.

00:35:00.490 --> 00:35:02.090
A couple of things about that--

00:35:02.090 --> 00:35:05.750
not all unimplemented sys calls
are a symptom that something

00:35:05.750 --> 00:35:07.050
is not working correctly.

00:35:07.050 --> 00:35:11.270
So I see set SOC ops is
one that is unimplemented

00:35:11.270 --> 00:35:14.360
for some of the arities
of that function call.

00:35:14.360 --> 00:35:16.880
And usually, that's
a red herring.

00:35:16.880 --> 00:35:18.770
It's not actually a problem.

00:35:18.770 --> 00:35:20.575
If you do find that
unimplemented calls

00:35:20.575 --> 00:35:22.700
are actually causing a
problem for you application.

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:24.200
We encourage you
to contact support.

00:35:24.200 --> 00:35:26.075
And we can work with
you to try to figure out

00:35:26.075 --> 00:35:27.374
exactly what's going on there.

00:35:32.497 --> 00:35:34.580
STEREN GIANNINI: You might
hit some, what we call,

00:35:34.580 --> 00:35:38.060
limits of the fully-managed
version of Cloud Run.

00:35:38.060 --> 00:35:42.350
For example, today, you cannot
pick more than 1 vCPU or more

00:35:42.350 --> 00:35:44.690
than 2 gigabytes of RAM.

00:35:44.690 --> 00:35:48.470
You do not have access
to a GPU on Cloud Run.

00:35:48.470 --> 00:35:50.990
We do not yet allow you
to connect to Cloud SQL

00:35:50.990 --> 00:35:52.650
or to connect to your VPC.

00:35:52.650 --> 00:35:55.940
But that's coming very soon.

00:35:55.940 --> 00:35:57.980
And as Mike mentioned,
we do not yet

00:35:57.980 --> 00:36:01.370
integrate with Google
Cloud Load Balancer.

00:36:01.370 --> 00:36:02.500
But you know what?

00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:08.330
We have another version of Cloud
Run, which is Cloud Run on GKE.

00:36:08.330 --> 00:36:11.900
So if you feel limited by
those restrictions, well,

00:36:11.900 --> 00:36:14.870
you can benefit from the
exact same Cloud Run developer

00:36:14.870 --> 00:36:18.730
experience, auto-scaling,
and automatic endpoints,

00:36:18.730 --> 00:36:21.290
but within your own GKE cluster.

00:36:21.290 --> 00:36:23.520
That is called Cloud Run on GKE.

00:36:23.520 --> 00:36:27.360
And the two have been designed
to expose the exact same API,

00:36:27.360 --> 00:36:28.970
which is the Knative API.

00:36:28.970 --> 00:36:31.440
It's super-easy to switch
between one or the other

00:36:31.440 --> 00:36:33.530
if you can change
your mind later.

00:36:33.530 --> 00:36:35.510
And really, it has
been designed so

00:36:35.510 --> 00:36:37.100
that, if you feel
limited by one,

00:36:37.100 --> 00:36:42.100
you can go to the other, in
your own cluster this time.

