00:40:11.130 --> 00:40:13.900
that I don't have to mention.

00:40:13.900 --> 00:40:17.150
Sebastien.

00:40:17.150 --> 00:40:19.280
SEBASTIEN MORAND:
Thank you, Antoine.

00:40:19.280 --> 00:40:22.280
So I am going to present
to you our use case.

00:40:22.280 --> 00:40:25.310
In our use case, Veolia is
managing a water treatment

00:40:25.310 --> 00:40:27.640
plant and [INAUDIBLE]
a lot of data.

00:40:27.640 --> 00:40:31.880
And data can be sensitive or
subject to local regulation.

00:40:31.880 --> 00:40:36.350
So we see for them with a
specific local algorithm,

00:40:36.350 --> 00:40:39.850
where the private key is
the only store on site.

00:40:39.850 --> 00:40:43.516
Then the date is ready
to go on Google Cloud.

00:40:43.516 --> 00:40:46.570
So on Google Cloud, we have
two layers of security.

00:40:46.570 --> 00:40:50.910
The first one is a native
Google Cloud Security layer,

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:54.450
and the second one is
the encryption on site.

00:40:54.450 --> 00:40:56.700
On Cloud, we are
going to make a lot

00:40:56.700 --> 00:41:01.080
of processing to make some
predictions for the local site.

00:41:01.080 --> 00:41:02.670
During the water
process, if someone

00:41:02.670 --> 00:41:05.820
tries to read the data,
even the local IT team

00:41:05.820 --> 00:41:08.460
managing the Google
Cloud Workload,

00:41:08.460 --> 00:41:11.220
you won't be able to read
the data, nor the data,

00:41:11.220 --> 00:41:13.560
nor the result. Because
it's [? ciphered ?]

00:41:13.560 --> 00:41:15.690
before the processing,
during the processing,

00:41:15.690 --> 00:41:17.310
and after the processing.

00:41:17.310 --> 00:41:20.970
Then the data is sent back to
the local site, and the client

00:41:20.970 --> 00:41:23.370
can [INAUDIBLE] the
data with a private key

00:41:23.370 --> 00:41:26.530
and get the insight of the data.

00:41:26.530 --> 00:41:28.020
So how to do that?

00:41:28.020 --> 00:41:31.740
To do that, we need first a
specific kind of algorithm

00:41:31.740 --> 00:41:33.900
called homomorphic algorithm.

00:41:33.900 --> 00:41:37.230
This kind of algorithm are
made from several trees

00:41:37.230 --> 00:41:39.900
from tens to
hundreds and provided

00:41:39.900 --> 00:41:42.960
by the CITECH Institute,
which is actually

00:41:42.960 --> 00:41:46.050
the Atomic Energy Commission
Research Technology

00:41:46.050 --> 00:41:49.090
center in France.

00:41:49.090 --> 00:41:51.960
So this is kind of
algorithm, first,

00:41:51.960 --> 00:41:54.660
we have to dispatch
the tree calculation

00:41:54.660 --> 00:41:58.840
with the first entry point
in our Cloud Run instance.

00:41:58.840 --> 00:42:01.180
Because actually,
the CITECH Institute

00:42:01.180 --> 00:42:03.290
provides only binaries,
and these binaries

00:42:03.290 --> 00:42:06.510
have been linked
into a Docker image.

00:42:06.510 --> 00:42:08.722
So when we publish the
message for each tree,

00:42:08.722 --> 00:42:11.480
we are going to trigger at
the same time all the tree

00:42:11.480 --> 00:42:12.960
calculations.

00:42:12.960 --> 00:42:18.023
And the result will be store
in a Cloud Firestore database.

00:42:18.023 --> 00:42:19.690
When all the trees
have been calculated,

00:42:19.690 --> 00:42:21.450
we calculate the
final step, which

00:42:21.450 --> 00:42:23.940
is called a median calculation,
to have the final result

00:42:23.940 --> 00:42:25.470
of the prediction.

00:42:25.470 --> 00:42:28.110
And the results will be
sent back to the client.

00:42:28.110 --> 00:42:29.990
During the whole process,
as I said before,

00:42:29.990 --> 00:42:33.945
the data is
[? ciphered ?] completely.

00:42:33.945 --> 00:42:36.880
So to implement the
use case, Cloud Run

00:42:36.880 --> 00:42:38.105
bring us a lot of benefits.

00:42:38.105 --> 00:42:41.220
The first one, of course,
as we have a Docker image

00:42:41.220 --> 00:42:44.460
with binaries, we
could plug as a service

00:42:44.460 --> 00:42:48.217
these Docker images
without any effort.

00:42:48.217 --> 00:42:49.800
The automatic scaling,
because we have

00:42:49.800 --> 00:42:52.110
a lot of trees to calculate--

00:42:52.110 --> 00:42:55.750
sometimes several hundreds--
they can last a few minutes.

00:42:55.750 --> 00:42:57.450
So calculating them
at the same time

00:42:57.450 --> 00:43:00.420
is really something required
to have a quick answer

00:43:00.420 --> 00:43:02.220
of the prediction.

00:43:02.220 --> 00:43:04.070
Multi-route support
because we need

00:43:04.070 --> 00:43:07.710
to dispatch the tree calculation
and the media calculation

00:43:07.710 --> 00:43:08.960
as the same Docker image.

00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:11.460
So through the multi-route,
we have only one deployment

00:43:11.460 --> 00:43:13.740
to maintain.

00:43:13.740 --> 00:43:16.080
Fast deployment is also
a mandatory feature,

00:43:16.080 --> 00:43:18.750
because when we want to make
an upgrade on the algorithm,

00:43:18.750 --> 00:43:23.040
we need to deploy quickly to
[? answer ?] the new feature

00:43:23.040 --> 00:43:25.530
we want to improve.

00:43:25.530 --> 00:43:27.488
And of course, the
last and not the least,

00:43:27.488 --> 00:43:29.280
we save money, because
we only pay for what

00:43:29.280 --> 00:43:31.960
we use during the processing.

00:43:31.960 --> 00:43:34.940
Thank you.

00:43:34.940 --> 00:43:37.400
STEREN GIANNINI: Thanks
to you for helping us

00:43:37.400 --> 00:43:39.908
during this alpha and
beta of Cloud Run.

00:43:39.908 --> 00:43:41.375
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:44.310 --> 00:43:47.100
Your feedback was really
valuable during that phase,

00:43:47.100 --> 00:43:50.940
and that's why we are very proud
to have you with us on stage

00:43:50.940 --> 00:43:52.920
today.

00:43:52.920 --> 00:43:56.350
OK, so as Sebastien
and Antoine mentioned,

00:43:56.350 --> 00:43:59.580
Google Cloud has a few other
serverless compute options.

00:43:59.580 --> 00:44:02.790
So I would like to spend
one minute helping you

00:44:02.790 --> 00:44:06.100
how to pick one option.

00:44:06.100 --> 00:44:07.980
It mostly depends on
the level of abstraction

00:44:07.980 --> 00:44:09.960
you want to position
yourself at.

00:44:09.960 --> 00:44:13.080
If you want to think in terms
of very simple function that are

00:44:13.080 --> 00:44:17.370
source based, that can be
triggered via HTTP but also

00:44:17.370 --> 00:44:22.080
by event triggers-- by
binding your function to some

00:44:22.080 --> 00:44:23.070
resources--

00:44:23.070 --> 00:44:26.110
then Cloud Functions is really
the perfect fit for that.

00:44:26.110 --> 00:44:26.790
It's GA.

00:44:26.790 --> 00:44:29.760
We announced many new
languages for Cloud Functions

00:44:29.760 --> 00:44:33.320
yesterday, many new
features coming.

00:44:33.320 --> 00:44:37.840
Really, serverless functions,
this is Cloud Functions.

00:44:37.840 --> 00:44:45.070
For use cases that are more
web apps or web backends,

00:44:45.070 --> 00:44:48.610
well, you also want to think
in terms of source code

00:44:48.610 --> 00:44:50.730
and not have to deal
with the container.

00:44:50.730 --> 00:44:54.220
Then App Engine would be the
best fit to run your serverless

00:44:54.220 --> 00:44:54.970
applications.

00:44:54.970 --> 00:44:58.900
So like Cloud Run, App Engine
scales up and down on demand

00:44:58.900 --> 00:45:01.310
and takes your
source code as input.

00:45:01.310 --> 00:45:03.770
We announced new
languages for App Engine's

00:45:03.770 --> 00:45:06.460
standard environment recently.

00:45:06.460 --> 00:45:09.880
And for anything else,
actually, that you

00:45:09.880 --> 00:45:14.410
want to run on a
serverless environment that

00:45:14.410 --> 00:45:18.010
is able to take as an
input an HTTP request,

00:45:18.010 --> 00:45:20.440
Cloud Run will be the
best fit for that.

00:45:23.320 --> 00:45:25.600
It is taking your
container image as an input

00:45:25.600 --> 00:45:28.120
and can be triggered
[? via HTTP, ?] but also,

00:45:28.120 --> 00:45:32.260
asynchronously using
Pub/Sub and Cloud Tasks.

00:45:32.260 --> 00:45:38.130
So Cloud Run, to recap,
containers to production

00:45:38.130 --> 00:45:39.780
in seconds.

00:45:39.780 --> 00:45:41.640
The benefit of
containers are multiple.

00:45:41.640 --> 00:45:45.270
You can put any
language, any binary

00:45:45.270 --> 00:45:48.810
and package this into
a container and deploy.

00:45:48.810 --> 00:45:51.650
It's very fast.

00:45:51.650 --> 00:45:55.610
So as you see, serverless is
not only for functions anymore.

00:45:55.610 --> 00:45:58.870
It takes a container and
runs it in a serverless way.

00:45:58.870 --> 00:46:01.810
And yes, Cloud Run is
natively serverless.

00:46:01.810 --> 00:46:05.350
As we demonstrated to you,
it scales up and down.

00:46:05.350 --> 00:46:10.510
You only pay for the exact usage
of your Cloud Run instances.

00:46:10.510 --> 00:46:12.950
And I haven't seen
any server to manage,

00:46:12.950 --> 00:46:16.490
so there is no infrastructure
management with Cloud Run.

00:46:16.490 --> 00:46:19.280
And lastly, we haven't put
so much emphasis on this,

00:46:19.280 --> 00:46:23.120
but Cloud Run gives you one
experience where you want it.

00:46:23.120 --> 00:46:25.070
So what we've talked
today is mostly

00:46:25.070 --> 00:46:28.460
about the fully-managed
version of Cloud Run.

00:46:28.460 --> 00:46:32.120
But I also mentioned
Cloud Run on GKE,

00:46:32.120 --> 00:46:33.620
which gives you
the same developer

00:46:33.620 --> 00:46:38.120
experience, but this time,
inside your GKE cluster.

00:46:38.120 --> 00:46:41.420
And as Mike mention,
all of this actually

00:46:41.420 --> 00:46:44.900
exposes the Knative Serving API.

00:46:44.900 --> 00:46:48.020
So thanks to that API
and runtime contract,

00:46:48.020 --> 00:46:50.780
you can actually run
Cloud Run workloads

00:46:50.780 --> 00:46:55.390
anywhere on Kubernetes where
you can install Knative.

00:46:55.390 --> 00:46:58.480
So that removes the vendor
[? lock-in ?] that you might be

00:46:58.480 --> 00:46:59.900
worried about.

00:46:59.900 --> 00:47:02.020
So these are the three
pillars of Cloud One--

00:47:02.020 --> 00:47:05.115
bringing serverless
agility to containers.

00:47:07.750 --> 00:47:10.480
If you want to learn more,
I have a few sessions

00:47:10.480 --> 00:47:11.650
to recommend.

00:47:11.650 --> 00:47:14.980
One of them is an intro on
what's new in serverless.

00:47:14.980 --> 00:47:18.820
Another one is an overview of
both Cloud Run and Cloud Run

00:47:18.820 --> 00:47:19.900
on GKE.

00:47:19.900 --> 00:47:22.990
And another one is a deep
dive similar to this one,

00:47:22.990 --> 00:47:27.970
except that a deep dive
on Cloud Run on GKE.

00:47:27.970 --> 00:47:32.868
If you want to ask us questions,
if you want to try the demo,

00:47:32.868 --> 00:47:33.910
all of this is on GitHub.

00:47:33.910 --> 00:47:36.370
And we are available
on Twitter, and you

00:47:36.370 --> 00:47:39.610
can follow us on GitHub, too.

00:47:39.610 --> 00:47:41.720
And lastly, I think,
Mike and I really

00:47:41.720 --> 00:47:46.530
want to thank you for
